WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.990
You know that your knowledge of a
language is kind of like an iceberg

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:09.030
in the sense that most of what
you know is beneath the surface.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.280
I like analogies.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:16.740
I think analogies give us a quick
sort of snapshot sense of a particular

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:21.450
phenomenon, and I sometimes twist the
analogy to suit what I'm trying to say.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.090
Of course an iceberg is not
similar to learning a language.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:33.180
You know, an iceberg is basically fresh
water that has calved, as they say, C

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:39.780
A L V E D, calf, you know, calf, calved
off, uh, you know, glacier or something.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.180
And because the, uh, freshwater
is, is lighter than the

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:46.560
saltwater, it floats and...

00:00:46.934 --> 00:00:48.824
but most of it is beneath the surface.

00:00:48.855 --> 00:00:53.144
You see a little bit on the top and
there's a lot below, and I see our

00:00:53.144 --> 00:00:55.995
language knowledge the same way.

00:00:56.595 --> 00:01:02.955
In other words, we have to have a lot
of familiarity with the language, some

00:01:02.955 --> 00:01:08.115
of which is passive vocabulary that
we're able to recognize when we see it.

00:01:08.354 --> 00:01:14.205
A lot more of it is vocabulary and
familiarity that we have piled into our

00:01:14.205 --> 00:01:20.055
brains through massive listening and
reading, and is sort of the base out

00:01:20.055 --> 00:01:24.615
of which will come eventually more and
more passive vocabulary, and eventually

00:01:24.615 --> 00:01:29.685
that smaller amount that we're actually
able to use our active vocabulary.

00:01:30.375 --> 00:01:33.825
Maybe a better analogy would be a tree.

00:01:34.695 --> 00:01:40.245
So some trees have shallow roots,
some trees have deeper roots.

00:01:40.305 --> 00:01:44.355
Not always, but typically a deeper
root could be deeper because

00:01:44.355 --> 00:01:45.795
it's searching for nutrients.

00:01:46.005 --> 00:01:50.265
Or, you know, different species
of trees tend to have shallower or

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:55.664
deeper roots, and they have, you know,
diagonal roots and typically, you

00:01:55.664 --> 00:02:01.140
know, the, uh, the deeper the root
system, the more the tree is able to

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.210
resist wind and so forth and so on.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.310
Again, it's not comparable.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:09.900
I prefer the iceberg analogy simply
because the tree ends up being an

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.780
awful lot larger than the root system.

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:19.980
However, the idea is that too much
of our language learning, sort of,

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:25.260
traditional instruction methods are aimed
at that little amount that we can see.

00:02:25.679 --> 00:02:29.459
So we try to get the student to produce
the language and produce it correctly,

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.829
or we, even with ourselves, we're more
concerned about what we're able to show

00:02:34.829 --> 00:02:39.660
off, you know, the smaller part of the
iceberg, and we don't give ourselves

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:44.130
enough credit for the size of that
iceberg that we have underneath it.

00:02:44.609 --> 00:02:48.810
Granted, my analogy doesn't work
because the iceberg is the iceberg.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.210
Once it's there, it's not
growing underneath the water.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.680
Whereas in language learning, you have
this constant ability to increase the

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:03.180
size of sort of the below the surface
familiarity, grasp, understanding

00:03:03.185 --> 00:03:07.590
of the language, accumulation of
bits of knowledge about the culture

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:10.079
of the language, about the history,
and all of these things that are

00:03:10.079 --> 00:03:15.090
growing underneath the surface, which
may not immediately be reflected in

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.760
your ability to use the language.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:25.410
But the stronger that below the surface,
uh, knowledge familiarity is the stronger

00:03:25.415 --> 00:03:30.180
the upper portion, the tree, to mix
you know, our analogy, is going to be

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:35.460
because the portion that sticks up, the
portion you can use, it's gonna fluctuate.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.730
If you don't use the language
for a while, you're gonna be not

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:40.080
as good at using the language.

00:03:40.755 --> 00:03:43.995
Uh, but if you have that solid
base, it'll come back very quickly.

00:03:44.115 --> 00:03:47.775
I just wanna use this sort of as a
quick analogy, although it doesn't

00:03:47.775 --> 00:03:49.995
really hold true in all aspects.

00:03:50.325 --> 00:03:55.215
This idea that if we focus more on
what we have below the surface in our

00:03:55.215 --> 00:03:59.985
language ability, if we accept the
fact and give ourselves credit for

00:04:00.165 --> 00:04:04.485
what we're building or what we have
already built below the surface, then

00:04:04.515 --> 00:04:09.480
we should be less concerned about what
we're able to use when we need it.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.910
Of course, we wanna be
able to use it well.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:18.329
And even if that little bit that sticks
up that's visible is ugly or jagged or

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:20.700
rough around the edges, it doesn't matter.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:21.839
That's what we got.

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:29.125
And if we continue using the bit that
we can see then if we have a solid base

00:04:29.215 --> 00:04:34.555
beneath us, we will gradually improve
and we can just to totally mix analogies

00:04:34.555 --> 00:04:39.385
here, we can sculpture that, uh, part
that's visible, make it better, make

00:04:39.385 --> 00:04:43.735
it smoother, make it more eloquent,
eloquent, rather make it, you know,

00:04:43.975 --> 00:04:45.805
more efficient in using the language.

00:04:45.805 --> 00:04:49.465
But it all assumes, uh,
that we have a solid base.

00:04:49.465 --> 00:04:51.625
So I just wanted to leave
that thought with you...

00:04:51.745 --> 00:04:52.075
with you.

00:04:52.075 --> 00:04:57.645
If you think of an iceberg, think of the
idea that you need a solid and broad base,

00:04:58.155 --> 00:05:02.925
some of which might be in the form of
passive vocabulary, some of which might

00:05:02.925 --> 00:05:07.784
just be in terms of having been exposed
to so much of the language patterns

00:05:07.784 --> 00:05:11.565
in the language, uh, bits and pieces
around the language and the culture.

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:15.930
All of these things support the bit
that we're able to use, and if we

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:19.950
have that solid base, we'll gradually
get better and better at using that

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.620
visible portion of the iceberg.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:28.410
I thought, I hope I haven't confused
you with my mixed parables or mixed

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:31.590
analogies here, or mixed metaphors.

